<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad al-Amin Bey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lamine Bey, Muhammad VIII al-Amin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> Bey, known as Lamine Bey, was the last ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husainid-dynasty">Husainid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Installed by the French in 1943 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moncef-bey">Moncef Bey</a> was deposed, he reigned as bey under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and became king when Tunisia gained independence in 1956. In 1957 the assembly led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habib-bourguiba">Habib Bourguiba</a> abolished the monarchy and placed him under house arrest.</p></div>
